  "query_preview": "..\n\nA region of showers and scattered thunderstorms across nrn Indiana \nis occuring within the lead ribbon of ascent tied to a remnant short \nwave. This wave is forecast to lift across the Toledo/western Lake \nErie region this evening into the overnight. This combined with \nsubtle upper divergence within the entrance region of an upper jet \nstreak will result in at least some degree of larger scale ascent \nbrushing across the eastern portions of the forecast area tonight. A \nchance of showers/thunderstorms therefore remains warranted over \nmuch of the area tonight. While cloud cover has held some limitation \non diurnal instability this afternoon, 0-1km ML CAPE around 1000 \nJ/kg is shown by latest meso analysis. Lake enchanted convergence \nacross the thumb and eastern sections of the forecast area remains \nsupportive of convective initiation late this afternoon and evening, \nwith a continued subtle mid level warm layer offering some \nlimitation on coverage.\n\nWhile large scale ascent will be lacking on Friday, model soundings \ncontinue to indicate an uncapped environment. Residual boundary \nlayer moisture will support sfc dewpoints near 70 with afternoon \ntemps forecast into the 80s. This will contribute increased daytime \nbuoyancy with various hi res models indicating SB CAPE ranging from \n1200 to 2k J/kg. Forcing via boundary layer convergence along sfc \ntroughs across Se Mi will support a chance for convection given the \nlack of capping. Although wet microbursts may contribute to some \nstrong storms, bulk shear values generally under 25 knots will limit \nthe severe risk. \n\nWhile there remains some timing differences, there is general model \nsupport in a progressive trough axis moving across srn Mi Fri \nnight/Saturday. Convective potential/coverage will have some \nreliance on timing as a later timing would offer the potential for \nthe associated ascent to coincde with increasing diurnal instability\nSaturday. Probabilistic guidance suggests enough timing uncertainty \nto maintain blanket chance pops through the Fri night/Saturday \nperiods. The trough and associated sfc front will support a push of \ndrier air into Se Mi in its wake. \n\nThe pattern is forecast to become more favorable for repeated MCS's \nacross the Great Lakes and/or Ohio Valley Sunday through Tuesday. \nThis will occur as the baroclinic zone and instability gradient sets \nup across the region. Fast mid level flow will favor forward \npropagating MCSs. However, there are indications for possible \nregenerative MCSs capable of producing locally heavy rainfall. At \nthis stage in the forecast, there is ample spread within the \nprobabilistic guidance with respect to QPF, timing and location. \nTrends will be monitored over the next couple days for both severe \nweather and heavy rainfall potential.",
